"value_qid":"Q345385","value_label":"Moktar Ould Daddah","from_t":1960.9071038251366,"to_t":1978.5205479452054,"title":"President of Mauritania","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Moktar Ould Daddah"},{"qid":"Q1025","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q451521","value_label":"Ely Ould Mohamed Vall","from_t":2005.5863013698631,"to_t":2007.295890410959,"title":"President of Mauritania","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Ely Ould Mohamed Vall"},{"qid":"Q1025","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q642826","value_label":"Mohamed Mahmoud Ould Louly","from_t":1979.4191780821918,"to_t":1980.0081967213114,"title":"President of Mauritania","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Mohamed Mahmoud Ould Louly"},{"qid":"Q1025","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q730612","value_label":"Mohamed Khouna Ould Haidalla","from_t":1980.0081967213114,"to_t":1984.9453551912568,"title":"President of Mauritania","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Mohamed Khouna Ould Haidalla"},{"qid":"Q1025","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q779723","value_label":"Mustafa Ould Salek","from_t":1978.5205479452054,"to_t":1979.4191780821918,"title":"President of Mauritania","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Mustafa Ould Salek"},{"qid":"Q1025","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q102977","value_label":"Flag of Mauritania.svg","from_t":2017.6191780821919,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Mauritania","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Mauritania"}],"people":{"Q12241106":{"label":"Mohamed Ould Ghazouani","description":"Mauritanian retired general and politician; President of Mauritania (2019–present)","enwiki":"Mohamed Ould Ghazouani","summary":"Mohamed Ould Cheikh Mohamed Ahmed Ould Ghazouani (born 4 December 1956), also known as Ghazouani and Ould Ghazouani, is a Mauritanian politician and retired army general who has been the 9th President of Mauritania since 2019, and the 22nd chairperson of the African Union from February 2024 until February 2025.\nGhazouani is a former general-director of National Security and former chief of staff of the Armed Forces of Mauritania (2008–2018). He was defense minister of Mauritania from October 2018 to March 2019. At that time a close ally of his predecessor Mohamed Ould Abdel Aziz, he was elected president of Mauritania on 22 June 2019 following the 2019 presidential election.","image":"President Mohamed Ould Ghazouani (2026-03-26) (cropped).jpg","birth":1956.9972677595629,"death":null},"Q66793577":{"label":"Moctar Ould Djay","description":"Mauritanian politician","enwiki":"Mokhtar Ould Djay","summary":"Mokhtar Ould Djay (Arabic: المختار ولد أجاي; born 28 December 1973), also spelled Moctar Ould Diay or Mokhtar Ould Diaye, is a Mauritanian politician serving as the Prime Minister of Mauritania since 2 August 2024.\nHe previously served as Minister of Finance and Minister of Economy and Finance under the presidency of Mohamed Ould Abdel Aziz, and more recently as Head of the Office of President Mohamed Ould Ghazouani.","image":"Moctar Ould Diay, Ouagadougou, 4 Feb 2019 (cropped).jpg","birth":1973.9890410958903,"death":null},"Q2877629":{"label":"Ba Mamadou Mbaré","description":"Mauritanian politician","enwiki":"Ba Mamadou Mbaré","summary":"Ba Mamadou dit Mbaré (1946 – 10 January 2013) was a Mauritanian politician who served as President of the Senate of Mauritania from 2006 until his death. As President of the Senate, he succeeded Mohamed Ould Abdel Aziz as Head of State on 15 April 2009, when Abdel Aziz resigned to take part in the June 2009 presidential election. Abdel Aziz was then elected president and in turn succeeded Mbaré on 5 August 2009.","image":null,"birth":1946,"death":2013.0246575342467},"Q294969":{"label":"Mohamed Ould Abdel Aziz","description":"President of Mauritania from 2009 to 2019","enwiki":"Mohamed Ould Abdel Aziz","summary":"Mohamed Ould Abdel Aziz (Hassaniyya: محمد ولد عبد العزيز; born 20 December 1956) is a retired Mauritanian military officer and politician who served as the 8th President of Mauritania from 2009 to 2019.\nA career soldier and high-ranking officer, he was a leading figure in the August 2005 coup that ousted President Maaouya Ould Sid'Ahmed Taya, and later in August 2008, he led another coup, that removed President Sidi Ould Cheikh Abdallahi. After the 2008 coup, Mohamed became president of the High Council of State as part of what was described as a political transition leading to a new election. He resigned from that post in April 2009 in order to stand as a candidate in the July 2009 presidential election, which he won.","image":"Mohamed Ould Abdel Aziz 2014-08-05.jpg","birth":1956.967213114754,"death":null},"Q310203":{"label":"Sidi Ould Cheikh Abdallahi","description":"Mauritanian politician (1938–2020)","enwiki":"Sidi Ould Cheikh Abdallahi","summary":"Sidi Mohamed Ould Cheikh Abdallahi (Arabic: سيدي محمد ولد الشيخ عبد الله‎; 1938 – 22 November 2020) was a Mauritanian politician who was President of Mauritania from 2007 to 2008. He served in the government during the 1970s, and after a long period of absence from politics he won the March 2007 presidential election, taking office on 19 April 2007. He was deposed in a military coup d'état on 6 August 2008.","image":"Sidi Mohamed Ould Cheikh Abdallahi.jpg","birth":1938,"death":2020.8934426229507},"Q313662":{"label":"Maaouya Ould Sid'Ahmed Taya","description":"President of Mauritania from 1984 to 2005","enwiki":"Maaouya Ould Sid'Ahmed Taya","summary":"Maaouya Ould Sid'Ahmed Ould Taya (born 28 November 1941) is a Mauritanian military officer and politician who served as the President of Mauritania from 1984 to 2005. He also served as the fifth Prime minister of Mauritania from 1981 to 1992 except for a brief period in 1984.\nBorn in Atar, French West Africa, Ould Taya studied at Franco-Arab school and then French military school. He participated in the Western Sahara War against the Polisario Front.","image":"Maaouya Ould Sid'Ahmed Taya (2005).jpg","birth":1941.9068493150685,"death":null},"Q345385":{"label":"Moktar Ould Daddah","description":"President of Mauritania from 1960 to 1978","enwiki":"Moktar Ould Daddah","summary":"Moktar Ould Daddah (Arabic: مختار ولد داداه, romanized: Mukhtār Wald Dāddāh; 25 December 1924 – 14 October 2003) was a Mauritanian politician who served as the country's first president after it gained its independence from France. Moktar served as the country's first Prime Minister from 1957 to 1961 and as its first President of Mauritania, a position he held for 18 years until he was deposed in a military coup d'etat in 1978.\nHe established an authoritarian one-party state, with his Mauritanian People's Party being the sole legal political entity in the country, and followed a policy of \"Islamic socialism\" with many nationalizations of private businesses. In his memoirs, Moktar expressed concern that the issue of slavery in Mauritania could lead to armed conflict that would ultimately destroy the country.","image":"Moktar Ould Daddah (I-008025, cropped).jpg","birth":1924.98087431694,"death":2003.7835616438356},"Q451521":{"label":"Ely Ould Mohamed Vall","description":"Mauritanian President (1953-2017)","enwiki":"Ely Ould Mohamed Vall","summary":"Colonel Ely Ould Mohamed Vall (Arabic: إعلي ولد محمد فال I‘lī Wald Muḥammad Fāl; 1953 – 5 May 2017) was a Mauritanian political and military figure. Following a coup d'état in August 2005, he served as the transitional military leader of Mauritania until 19 April 2007, when he relinquished power to an elected government.","image":"Visit by Ely Ould Mohamed Vall, President of Mauritania, to the EC - 2006.jpg","birth":1953,"death":2017.3397260273973},"Q642826":{"label":"Mohamed Mahmoud Ould Louly","description":"President of Mauritania (1943-2019)","enwiki":"Mohamed Mahmoud Ould Louly","summary":"Lt. Col. Mohamed Mahmoud Ould Louly (Arabic: محمد محمود ولد أحمد لولي‎; 1 January 1943 – 16 March 2019) was a Mauritanian military officer and politician who served as the President of Mauritania and Chairman of the Military Committee for National Salvation (CMSN) from 3 June 1979 to 4 January 1980.","image":"Mohamed Mahmoud Ould Louly illustration.png","birth":1943,"death":2019.2027397260274},"Q730612":{"label":"Mohamed Khouna Ould Haidalla","description":"former head of state of Mauritania","enwiki":"Mohamed Khouna Ould Haidalla","summary":"Colonel Mohamed Khouna Ould Haidalla (Arabic: محمد خونا ولد هيداله Muḥammad Khouna Wald Haidalla; born 1940) is a Mauritanian former military officer and politician who served as  the head of state of Mauritania from 4 January 1980 to 12 December 1984.\nHe was an unsuccessful candidate in the 2003 presidential election and the 2007 presidential election.","image":"Mohamed Khouna 1983 (profile).jpg","birth":1940,"death":null},"Q779723":{"label":"Mustafa Ould Salek","description":"Mauritanian politician (1936–2012)","enwiki":"Mustafa Ould Salek","summary":"Col. Mustafa Ould Salek (Arabic: المصطفى ولد محمد السالك; 1936 – 18 December 2012) was the president of Mauritania from 1978 to 1979.","image":"Ould Mohamed Saleck, 1978.png","birth":1936,"death":2012.9617486338798}},"media":{"Flag of Mauritania.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Flag_of_Mauritania.svg/330px-Flag_of_Mauritania.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Mauritania.svg","author":"See File history below for details.","license":"Public domain"},"National Seal of Mauritania.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/National_Seal_of_Mauritania.svg/330px-National_Seal_of_Mauritania.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Seal_of_Mauritania.svg","author":"Jelloud Najem","license":"Public domain"},"President Mohamed Ould Ghazouani (2026-03-26) (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb